---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { implementationDoc, specificationDoc } from '../../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="Graph retrieval in sempods — find a foothold, follow the links"
  description="Use find to locate resources, follow their RDF links, and build a useful graph within the caller's authorized data."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts · retrieval</p>
  <h1>A foothold is more useful than a dump</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An event has a name, dates, a place and links. A task has people and
    projects around it. Retrieval can begin with one useful resource and
    follow those relationships until the client has what its task needs.
  </p>
  <pre class="ascii-diagram" role="img" data-no-copy aria-label="Find a starting resource, follow links, then build a view or answer">{`question or view
      |
      v
     find
      |
      v
 working graph ----> view or answer
      |
      +--> follow links
           resource reads / SPARQL`}</pre>
  <h2>Find is the entry point</h2>
  <p>
    A client gives <code>find</code> text and optional constraints such as
    resource type. It receives an RDF subgraph with matching resources and
    useful surrounding statements. The result gives it things to inspect
    and addresses to follow.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contract describes the result rather than a search algorithm. Kotlin
    0.2 supplies a SPARQL regex adapter over literals. A deployment can add
    other adapters behind the same interface; vector retrieval is not a
    feature shipped by that adapter.
  </p>
  <h2>Traversal does the deeper work</h2>
  <p>
    Fetch a known resource, follow its links, or query the neighbourhood
    you need. Add those facts to the working graph. A calendar can render it;
    an agent can use it to answer a question.
  </p>
  <p>
    This loop is client behavior built on the data operations. The client
    chooses when it has enough information, while each pod decides which
    facts it may return.
  </p>
  <h2>Permissions apply throughout</h2>
  <p>
    The current contract limits retrieval and traversal to readable Contexts.
    An anonymous visitor can find public events. A caller with private grants
    can reach more. Search cannot bypass the authorization decision made for
    the same data through a resource read or SPARQL.
  </p>
  <h2>Useful for memory, useful for ordinary apps</h2>
  <p>
    Notes, tasks and observations could become linked knowledge in a personal
    pod. An agent could gather the relevant slice for one question without
    making its own product the permanent home of that memory.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same pattern helps a venue website fetch a location for an event.
    Shared meaning and stable addresses make the links useful to both clients.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/core/find.md')}>Find contract</a>{' '}
    <a href={implementationDoc('docs/concepts/graph-retrieval.md')}>Implementation and adapters</a>{' '}
    <a href="/concepts/mcp-over-pods">Use the loop through MCP</a>{' '}
    <a href="/start#build">Build a client</a>
  </p>
</Base>
